[[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] is the economic centre of the comarca, with its economy dominated by the operation of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] hydroelectric facility. The town was founded as a Volisanian trading post in 1625 and served as the comarca capital until 1970, when the capital was moved to the relocated village of Vila de Llevant as part of the relocation agreement associated with the dam's construction.

[[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]] is the administrative centre of the comarca, situated on the eastern shore of Lake Iwarakí. The village was relocated to its present site in 1970 from its original location on the former shoreline of the natural lake, which was submerged by the rising water level following the construction of the Gran Presa del Nord. As part of the relocation agreement with the [[Birú people|Birú]] community, the comarca capital was moved from Sant Jorge to the relocated village, and the government committed to providing public sector employment to all original residents and their descendants.

The remaining population is distributed across small rural communities and Birú settlements in the highland valleys and slopes, engaged primarily in subsistence agriculture.

== History ==

The area of La Serra de Llevant has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The Birú retreated into the northern highlands following the Volisanian conquest of the isthmian coast and maintained communities in relative isolation throughout the colonial period. The Birú population relied on the highland valleys for agriculture, the lakes and streams for fishing, and the limestone caves for ceremonial purposes. The region's remoteness and difficult terrain limited colonial authority to occasional visits by provincial officials and clergy, allowing the Birú to maintain a degree of autonomy through the colonial and republican periods.

A Volisanian trading post was established at the site of present-day [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] in 1625, serving as a minor node in the colonial highland trade network. The settlement remained small throughout the colonial and republican periods, with limited growth and minimal integration into the broader colonial economy. The expansion of cattle ranching and logging into the highland valleys in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries gradually encroached on traditional Birú lands.

The [[Sant Jorge Airport]] was constructed in 1928, providing the first reliable year-round transport link to the area and replacing the seasonal road access that had previously been the only connection to the provincial network.

=== Gran Presa del Nord and the relocation of Vila de Llevant ===

{{main|Gran Presa del Nord|Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana}}

In 1970, the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) began construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] on the southern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]]. The dam, completed in 1979, was one of the largest infrastructure projects undertaken in Tramuntana Province during the post-reunification hydroelectric expansion. The project raised the water level of the natural lake, converting it into a regulated reservoir, and required the relocation of the Birú village of [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]] from its original site on the former shoreline to higher ground on the eastern shore.

The relocation order prompted protests and confrontations between the Birú community and government authorities, as the original village site held cultural and historical significance. The government funded the relocation, and as part of the relocation agreement, the comarca capital was moved from [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] to the relocated Vila de Llevant, and the government committed to providing public sector employment to all original residents and their descendants.

The dam project transformed Sant Jorge from a small agricultural town into the primary service centre for the comarca, as the CHB facility became the town's primary employer. The construction brought an influx of workers and engineers to the area, and the subsequent operation of the dam and its hydroelectric station provided stable, skilled employment.

The displacement of Vila de Llevant was a source of lasting grievance among the Birú communities in the comarca and contributed to the broader opposition of indigenous groups to subsequent hydroelectric development in the Yalabirga highlands. Opposition from environmental groups and Birú communities to further dam construction in the highlands contributed to the government's decision to pursue nuclear power through the establishment of [[Organització d'Energia Nuclear de Balboa|OENB]] in 1998.

=== 2021 Carretera Gran Presa del Nord protest ===

{{main|2021 Carretera Gran Presa del Nord protest}}

On 8 March 2021, the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB) organised a sit-in protest at the construction site of the Nou Gran Presa Carretera, a proposed road connecting the [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]] to the [[Gran Presa del Nord]]. The road's planned route required a tunnel through the limestone formation containing the Birú sacred caves on the eastern slope of the Yalabirga range. The protest ended in violence when a confrontation between protesters and private security guards employed by [[Nivell de Seguretat]] resulted in the kidnapping and killing of [[Hernandez Lee]], a 23-year-old Zong-Balboan MPB member, by NdS guard [[Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal]].

The killing triggered a national media controversy and led to the suspension of the road project. The project was subsequently redesigned with a revised route that avoided the sacred caves entirely, following consultation with Birú community representatives. The revised route was approved in 2023, and construction of the redesigned road began in 2024. The family of Lee filed a civil lawsuit against Nivell de Seguretat, but the company was found not liable in 2023.

=== 2026 data center protests ===

{{main|2026 Balboa data center protests}}

In August 2026, [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] (NTE) commenced construction of a data center facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] on lands traditionally used by highland Birú communities. The site, located in the La Serra de Llevant comarca, had been the subject of sustained opposition from Birú communities and the MPB since its proposal earlier in the year. NTE broke ground on 18 August 2026, triggering nationwide protests that lasted from 18 to 27 August. The protests, coordinated by the MPB and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB), centred on the Sant Jorge site and spread to cities across the country. Construction was halted on 27 August pending a government review of the project's environmental and social impact.

== Economy ==

The economy of La Serra de Llevant is dominated by the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB), which operates the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] hydroelectric dam and power station on [[Lake Iwarakí]]. CHB is the comarca's primary employer, providing skilled and semi-skilled positions in dam operation, maintenance, administration, and environmental monitoring. The presence of the CHB facility has made [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] the economic centre of the comarca, supporting a range of service businesses that cater to the dam workforce and their families.

Agriculture remains an important secondary economic activity. The valley floors and surrounding highland slopes support the cultivation of maize, beans, root vegetables, and coffee, primarily for local consumption and regional sale. Small mining operations in the surrounding highlands extract limited quantities of minerals, providing supplementary employment for the local population.

The comarca has a minor tourism sector centred on Lake Iwarakí, attracting visitors drawn by the lake's scenic setting in the highlands and its still, mirror-like surface during the dry season. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]] and the lake are the principal attractions, though the area's remoteness and limited transport infrastructure constrain visitor numbers. The Birú highland communities have developed a small but growing cultural tourism sector, offering guided visits to traditional villages and artisan workshops, though the sector remains limited by infrastructure and the remoteness of the communities.

The public sector is a significant employer in [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]], where the municipal council, comarca administrative offices, health clinic, and primary school provide employment under the 1970 relocation agreement. The provincial government has identified La Serra de Llevant as a priority area for development assistance under the national rural development programme.

== Demographics ==

La Serra de Llevant had a population of 25,000 in 2026, making it one of the most sparsely populated comarcas in Balboa. The population is concentrated in the two principal settlements — [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] (10,000) and [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]] (7,000) — with the remainder distributed across small rural communities and Birú settlements in the highland valleys and slopes.

The comarca has a significant [[Birú people|Birú]] population, one of the highest proportions of any comarca in Tramuntana Province. The Birú communities in the comarca are concentrated in the rural highland areas, particularly in the valleys and slopes surrounding Lake Iwarakí and along the eastern ridgelines of the Yalabirga range. The Birú population has maintained a degree of cultural continuity, though the [[Birú Dale language]] is critically endangered, and most Birú under the age of fifty speak Volisanian or Balboan Creole as their first language.

The comarca also has a [[White Balboan people|white Balboan]] population, concentrated in Sant Jorge and the surrounding agricultural valleys, descended from Volisanian settlers and later immigrants who arrived under the post-independence settlement programmes. The population of Vila de Llevant is predominantly Birú.

== Transport ==

The [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]] is the primary road link across the northern highlands and passes through [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]], providing a route south to [[Portblanc]], the provincial capital, and northwest to the neighbouring [[La Taula]] comarca. A regional road links Sant Jorge to [[Sant Martí del Nord, Tramuntana|Sant Martí del Nord]], the capital of La Taula. Roads in the comarca are narrow and winding, reflecting the rugged highland terrain, and are frequently affected by landslides during the wet season.

[[Sant Jorge Airport]] (IATA: SJD, ICAO: SJIB), constructed in 1928, is a domestic turboprop-only airport located near Sant Jorge. The airport is served by [[Aeroístmus]] from [[Portblanc Airport|Portblanc]] as a stop on the route from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]], operating under the national essential services programme. The airport handled approximately 55,000 passengers in 2025.

== Indigenous rights and environmental conflict ==

La Serra de Llevant has been a focal point of indigenous rights activism and environmental conflict in Balboa since the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] in the 1970s. The comarca's Birú communities have opposed a series of infrastructure projects on traditional lands, including the dam itself, the Nou Gran Presa Carretera road project, and the [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] data center facility.

The [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB), a decentralised anarcho-syndicalist movement that emerged in the early 2020s, has its organisational base in the Birú communities of the comarca and the neighbouring highland areas. The MPB organised both the [[2021 Carretera Gran Presa del Nord protest]] and the [[2026 Balboa data center protests|2026 data center protests]], and the comarca has been the site of the most significant confrontations between indigenous rights activists and private security forces in Balboa's recent history.

The killing of [[Hernandez Lee]] during the 2021 protest and the subsequent civil lawsuit against [[Nivell de Seguretat]] — in which the company was found not liable — became a rallying point for the indigenous rights movement and was cited during the 2026 data center protests as evidence of the perceived impunity of private security firms operating at protest sites.

